svga: send every constant buffer binding again before the device's copy
of it can be recycled.

VirtualBox's DX device does not read a constant buffer binding when a
draw runs.  It copies the bound range out of the buffer at the moment
SET_SINGLE_CONSTANT_BUFFER (or SET_*_CONSTANT_BUFFER_OFFSET) arrives,
into a ring of 512 KB buffers of its own, and hands a ring buffer out
again as soon as the GPU has finished with it -- whether or not a binding
still points into it.

A stage whose constants never change is bound once and never again.
Another stage's per-draw offset changes keep filling the ring meanwhile
(one copy per draw).  Once the ring has come round, the unchanged stage
reads whatever was copied into its old place since.  WebKit's compositor
is exactly that case: its vertex shader gets a new transform per tile,
its pixel shader keeps one colour matrix for seconds on end, and when
the matrix is overwritten by some tile's transform the whole web view
turns yellow or pink (red and green constant, blue still the page) until
the pixel shader's constants happen to change.

The driver now counts the bytes the device copies (rounded as it rounds
them) and, once half a ring buffer's worth has been copied, sends every
binding again in full on the next state update.  A copy is recycled only
after the rest of its buffer and the whole of the next one have been
filled, so every live binding's copy stays newer than that.  Devices that
read bindings at draw time just see the same binding once more.

